## PROJECT FRAMING -- a defensive software-robustness project

GXemul is a computer-architecture emulator: a developer tool that runs legacy operating systems in a software sandbox for research
and preservation.  This fork is a HARDENING effort -- ordinary software-correctness engineering on the emulator's own C code: finding
and fixing out-of-bounds array accesses, missing bounds checks, unchecked lengths and crash-on-malformed-input defects in the emulated
device models, and shipping regression tests that keep them fixed.  There is no attack, no target system and no affected third party;
the 'guest' is an operating-system image under test inside the emulator, and the 'host' is the emulator process on the developer's own
machine.  A 'test variant' is a deliberately broken copy of a fix used in mutation testing to measure whether the regression suite
notices the breakage; 'detect' means a regression row turns red.  Every question below is about making this emulator's code correct
and its tests complete.

# qhslowifetch REPAIR PASS 6 -- the repair-6 diff (detector only: two rows' seed, one new session, the records)

## READ THIS FIRST -- PLEASE DO NOT SIMPLY RATIFY

A recent panel had seven of nine seats confirm a premise its brief stated wrongly.  **Check the premises, not just the
derivations.**  Where I state something as fact, ask whether it is true; a seat that re-derives a premise from something this
packet did NOT give it is worth more than a seat that agrees.  **The tree under review is `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`** (the hardened
fork); pristine 2021-upstream copies of GXemul exist elsewhere and are NOT this fork.  Every fact you need is inlined; name any
premise you cannot settle from this packet rather than guessing.  Where an arithmetic claim appears (an offset, a count, a
range), ENUMERATE it rather than reasoning about it.  **VERDICT LINE FIRST.**

## THE STOP CONDITION THIS PASS IS JUDGED BY (the project's standing rule, quoted so you can hold me to it)

A repair made in response to a pass 2 owes its own pass.  The chain closes on the FIRST VALID PASS THAT FINDS ZERO FLOOR BREACHES
SURVIVING ADJUDICATION.  A closing pass must be valid: the diff actually delivered (it is, below), and the zero carried WITH ITS
DENOMINATOR -- which cells and which edit shapes you swept.  "Zero found" and "zero looked for" must not read the same.  Review
EFFORT is scoped to the repair diff (settled design is not re-argued); the FLOOR QUESTION -- does a single edit inside the bound
keep every row green -- is asked of the SHIPPED COMPOSITE detector.  THE BOUND (DEPENDS-ON): the predicates dominating the fix's
edited lines, walked up only to the enclosing function boundary, never into callees or callers; operand swaps drawn only from
fields the guarded body reads; joint cells only where the body's own tests distinguish them.  A filter may reject or repair a row;
it never demands rows outside that bound.  A finding that is not a floor breach or a false record is FILED, not fixed now.

## 0. What this pass reviews

Pass 5 (GPT-6 Astra, glm-5.3, Mythos 5.1 x3, Gemini 4 Argon x2) agreed the repair-5 PRODUCT change is right and found two floor
breaches, both MEASURED before this repair (section 3): (1) the aligned compare's mask WIDENED to bit 2 (`& ~7`) scored 79/79,
because T blr's r3 = 0x02040003 has bit 2 clear (codex, glm, Mythos x3); (2) arm (a)'s guard CONJOINED with an alignment test
(`&& cpu->pc == addr`, `&& !(cpu->pc & 3)`) scored 79/79, because every measured read-ahead started from an aligned pc (Mythos r1,
r2).  Both are floor breaches repaired in-round (L2, no new ruling): T blr re-seeded at r3 = 0x02040007 (bits 0-2 set; addr
0x02040004), and a `T ra odd` session (the T ra continue entered at 0xfec00005) pinning exit 0, pc 0xfec00009 and two reports.
Records: the wrong record Mythos r3 found (THE FIX said arm (c) parks 'at the failing address'; the park is at the fetch's pc)
corrected; disclosures added for what the seats named and no row can see (glm's `|| translation_readahead` on the failure test:
throughput only; codex's `|| cpu->pc != addr` on the failure test: FILED; a mask clearing a bit above bit 3 while keeping bits
2-3: not claimed); the mtlr/bclr wording made exact (only the unconditional blr, X(bclr_20), copies LR unmasked).  The product
is UNCHANGED since repair 5 (section 1 is the failure block as it stands).  Gemini's pass-5 readings found nothing beyond codex.

## 1. The fetch prologue and the failure block (src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c, X(to_be_translated), AS IT NOW STANDS)

